    "result_text": "The change is fully wired through. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe task: return `riskFlag` `true` when the owner is a possible duplicate, the email domain is disposable-adjacent, or the city is over its soft capacity; otherwise `false`.\n\n`riskFlag` is a read-derived response field, so I followed the same pattern the codebase already uses for other computed Owner fields (`contactPreference`, `capacityWarning`, etc.): declare it in the OpenAPI schema (which generates the DTO) and compute it in `OwnerMapper`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `riskFlag` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, documenting the three trigger conditions.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added:\n   - `@Mapping(target = \"riskFlag\", expression = \"java(riskFlag(owner))\")` on `toOwnerDto`.\n   - `riskFlag(Owner)` \u2014 returns `true` if any of: `possibleDuplicate` is true, the email is disposable-adjacent, or `capacityWarning` is true (the snapshotted \"over soft capacity\" indicator, since capacity-warning fires at 40\u201349 owners, the soft threshold below the hard cap of 50).\n   - `isDisposableAdjacentEmail(String)` + a `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_LABELS` set. A domain is disposable-adjacent when its second-level label matches a known disposable provider (`mailinator`, `tempmail`, `guerrillamail`) \u2014 catching related domains that aren't exact blocklist entries (a different TLD like `mailinator.net`, or a subdomain like `inbox.mailinator.com`). Exact blocklist domains are already rejected at create time with a 400, so this softer check is the meaningful one for created owners.\n\n**Tests:** The full suite is green \u2014 `AcceptanceTest.coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` (a unique owner with no email \u2192 `riskFlag` false) passes, and `BUILD SUCCESS`. I verified the generated DTO and MapStruct impl both include the new field.\n\nOne note on interpretation: the exact definition of \"disposable-adjacent\" wasn't specified anywhere in the repo (no spec file beyond the one-line task, and the only test covers the `false` case). I chose a deterministic second-level-label match against the existing disposable blocklist, since those exact domains are already rejected at create \u2014 making \"adjacent\" the natural remaining category. I documented this precisely in both the schema and the mapper Javadoc.",
